WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:06.999
&gt;&gt;Thank you chair.

00:00:06.999 --> 00:00:15.160
So, this paper sets out some work that we
have been doing with CCGs about how NHS England

00:00:15.160 --> 00:00:21.449
and others can support CCGs in their development,
and CCGs as new organisations.

00:00:21.449 --> 00:00:29.429
We recognise that authorisation gave them
a safe threshold to operate, but to deliver

00:00:29.429 --> 00:00:36.000
the challenges that we face there is a further
development journey for CCGs.

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:41.230
We have done it together, and I am very grateful
to Nadim Fazlani, who is the Clinical Chair

00:00:41.230 --> 00:00:46.429
of Liverpool CCG, who has been co-chairing
the development working group with John Bewick

00:00:46.429 --> 00:00:47.429
from our team.

00:00:47.429 --> 00:00:53.230
We have had over 160 CCGs be involved in putting
this work together.

00:00:53.230 --> 00:00:57.070
I think there are three things that I would
pull out from it.

00:00:57.070 --> 00:01:04.040
First, it is built on insight and analysis
about what are the biggest development needs

00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:07.640
in order to meet the challenges that we face.

00:01:07.640 --> 00:01:13.820
Secondly, we hope that it enables suppliers
of development support to put their efforts

00:01:13.820 --> 00:01:18.680
in the right place, so not spending time putting
offers into the market which are not the things

00:01:18.680 --> 00:01:21.390
that the system most needs.

00:01:21.390 --> 00:01:27.050
Thirdly, it will begin to guide us in NHS
England about how we ourselves provide support

00:01:27.050 --> 00:01:34.119
to CCGs, whether that is through directly
helping, which, as we went through authorisation,

00:01:34.119 --> 00:01:39.799
in some parts of the country we have been
giving direct help to CCGs, but also in facilitating

00:01:39.799 --> 00:01:49.020
networks in order that you get peer to peer
learning and guidance, and bringing international

00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:51.280
knowledge and insight to bear.

00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:54.159
So, this work is not set in stone.

00:01:54.159 --> 00:02:01.220
We continue to work and develop it in light
of the context as it changes, and I would

00:02:01.220 --> 00:02:05.430
like to invite the Board to comment and endorse
the approach set out.

00:02:05.430 --> 00:02:19.680
&gt;&gt; If I can just ask a slightly more open
question: how are CCGs feeling and how are

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:23.810
they feeling about the relationship with our
local area teams and with NHS England?

00:02:23.810 --> 00:02:30.310
&gt;&gt; Well, I do not know whether Barbara wants
to comment on the area teams, but I would

00:02:30.310 --> 00:02:36.340
say at a national level we have put a lot
of work into building a shared view about

00:02:36.340 --> 00:02:38.280
how the relationships would operate.

00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:46.040
Last week, we published values that would
underpin our working relationships in the

00:02:46.040 --> 00:02:53.720
three areas that we work, assurance and oversight,
development and co-commissioning, because

00:02:53.720 --> 00:02:59.900
sometimes NHS England are sat alongside CCGs
in discussions.

00:02:59.900 --> 00:03:06.319
What we have found is that some of the practical
issues of transitions, in working through

00:03:06.319 --> 00:03:11.900
that over the last six months, there has been
that time which is inevitable, I think, in

00:03:11.900 --> 00:03:18.069
new systems of people getting to know each
other and understanding how to work together.

00:03:18.069 --> 00:03:24.709
I think we continue to work on that; we are
doing a 360 survey, so NHS Clinical Commissioners,

00:03:24.709 --> 00:03:31.080
a national body, will be doing a survey of
CCGs to tell us what they think in the autumn.

00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:36.030
So, then the Board will actually have some
very concrete data as part of our organisational

00:03:36.030 --> 00:03:38.510
health, so it will be more than anecdote.

00:03:38.510 --> 00:03:44.409
&gt;&gt; I do think this is good.

00:03:44.409 --> 00:03:47.470
I think everybody knows how critical this
is in the light of the conversation we had

00:03:47.470 --> 00:03:53.049
about the spending review; this is where the
tyres hit tarmac, so it is mission critical

00:03:53.049 --> 00:03:58.830
that we get it right.

00:03:58.830 --> 00:04:03.870
To some degree, CCGs do not know what they
do not know; some of these people are taking

00:04:03.870 --> 00:04:12.459
on leadership positions that they are not
used to, and are really challenged with how

00:04:12.459 --> 00:04:21.420
they create the right relationships and have
the right conversations to move things forward.

00:04:21.420 --> 00:04:26.860
You do mention this in the paper, but I just
want to emphasise the need for support in

00:04:26.860 --> 00:04:33.480
helping people diagnose what the issues might
be before moving into contracting with any

00:04:33.480 --> 00:04:40.570
kind of intervention and then implementation
and review almost in each case.

00:04:40.570 --> 00:04:48.350
I think what we are asking people to do at
CCG leadership level is shift their thinking

00:04:48.350 --> 00:04:52.910
and their doing from where they were, but
we are not quite sure where it needs them

00:04:52.910 --> 00:04:53.910
to be.

00:04:53.910 --> 00:05:01.260
We are needing to encourage a very different
type of leadership to the kind that they have

00:05:01.260 --> 00:05:10.530
experienced or delivered in the past, and
I think that I would like more emphasis on

00:05:10.530 --> 00:05:16.220
how we do that, how we measure whether we
are doing it and what the interventions might

00:05:16.220 --> 00:05:17.740
be that are available.

00:05:17.740 --> 00:05:26.540
I still wonder about to what extent this has
been truly jointly created with CCGs; I can

00:05:26.540 --> 00:05:33.270
see it in the paper, but I cannot emphasise
enough how important it is that they are allowed

00:05:33.270 --> 00:05:34.540
to say, 'You know what?

00:05:34.540 --> 00:05:36.650
I am really struggling with the relationship
here.

00:05:36.650 --> 00:05:42.240
I do not quite know what is wrong; I need
help to diagnose the problem across my local

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:45.090
system and in my relationships.'

00:05:45.090 --> 00:05:54.180
That, I think, will be critical going forward.

00:05:54.180 --> 00:06:01.390
&gt;&gt; Just to comment on that, certainly CCGs
I hope have really great relationships with

00:06:01.390 --> 00:06:02.680
their area teams.

00:06:02.680 --> 00:06:08.930
It is clear that all have good relationships
but some are better than others, and I think

00:06:08.930 --> 00:06:14.130
that in terms of that day to day on-going
relationship we should not underestimate the

00:06:14.130 --> 00:06:19.240
degree of contact that there is between the
average area team and its small number of

00:06:19.240 --> 00:06:25.680
CCGs on a very regular basis, which I think
will put into place some of the informed discussions

00:06:25.680 --> 00:06:30.810
you are talking about, about helping to diagnose
and identify where they can get further support,

00:06:30.810 --> 00:06:34.030
even for those CCGs which are authorised without
conditions.

00:06:34.030 --> 00:06:40.760
Plainly, the next more official or formal
step to that is the CCG assurance process,

00:06:40.760 --> 00:06:43.980
and as you know we are out consulting on that
at the moment; the Board has had a copy and

00:06:43.980 --> 00:06:47.680
we will be talking about that I think at the
next Board meeting.

00:06:47.680 --> 00:06:53.780
That will be the opportunity for CCGs to not
have as comprehensive a process as we did

00:06:53.780 --> 00:06:57.430
in authorisation, but nonetheless to have
a very significant process that allows us

00:06:57.430 --> 00:07:14.030
to look at the range of competence and ability
and then the sort of support that the CCGs

00:07:14.030 --> 00:07:16.610
need.

00:07:16.610 --> 00:07:23.080
&gt;&gt; Thank you very much; thank you, Ros.

00:07:23.080 --> 00:07:27.460
&gt;&gt;The subject of genomics I think has come
of age.

00:07:27.460 --> 00:07:30.950
Many of you will remember that only a few
years ago there was a race to sequence the

00:07:30.950 --> 00:07:39.020
human genome, and since then the cost of sequencing
the human genome has dropped from well over

00:07:39.020 --> 00:07:41.780
$1 million down to about $1,000.

00:07:41.780 --> 00:07:46.100
The time to do it has fallen from months down
to about a day.

00:07:46.100 --> 00:07:51.790
Our understanding of the human genome has
increased quite significantly.

00:07:51.790 --> 00:07:58.140
This provides a whole vista of new opportunities
for us, first of all for our patients, secondly

00:07:58.140 --> 00:08:03.010
for academia in this country and thirdly for
UK plc.

00:08:03.010 --> 00:08:10.580
So, for patients, it was not long ago that,
if you developed breast cancer, for example,

00:08:10.580 --> 00:08:17.540
there were only three different types; now
there are well over 40 different types and

00:08:17.540 --> 00:08:23.240
the treatment is targeted according to certain
bits of genetic sequencing within the tumour,

00:08:23.240 --> 00:08:29.370
which means that different people get different
treatments which are more effective for them

00:08:29.370 --> 00:08:31.300
individually.

00:08:31.300 --> 00:08:38.130
In the case of some types of lung cancer the
treatment is entirely guided now by the genetic

00:08:38.130 --> 00:08:41.130
sequence of parts of the human genome.

00:08:41.130 --> 00:08:45.800
What we can do now is not just look at parts
of the human genome: we can look at the whole

00:08:45.800 --> 00:08:55.070
thing, and that provides some big opportunities
for academic colleagues in the United Kingdom

00:08:55.070 --> 00:09:00.230
because it provides an opportunity to start
to explore the human genome, specifically

00:09:00.230 --> 00:09:06.590
by linking what we see in the genome to the
characteristics of individuals and diseases

00:09:06.590 --> 00:09:10.510
that they may or may not suffer from.

00:09:10.510 --> 00:09:17.640
I think our NHS is particularly well equipped
to take advantage of some of this because

00:09:17.640 --> 00:09:22.660
we are one of the biggest integrated healthcare
systems in the world.

00:09:22.660 --> 00:09:30.580
We have the ability to put things in place
throughout our system, which can bring economies

00:09:30.580 --> 00:09:36.630
of scale when it comes to trying to address
some of these issues.

00:09:36.630 --> 00:09:42.240
So, the Department of Health some time ago
had started to think about this and developed

00:09:42.240 --> 00:09:51.450
a human genome strategy group to think about
how we might start to configure services for

00:09:51.450 --> 00:09:56.480
delivering this; this is laboratory services,
really, that I am talking about.

00:09:56.480 --> 00:10:03.990
Since then, the Prime Minister threw out a
challenge that we should be the first healthcare

00:10:03.990 --> 00:10:10.150
system in the world to characterise 100,000
genomes.

00:10:10.150 --> 00:10:19.490
We have set about that and this paper describes,
really, how we are setting about doing that

00:10:19.490 --> 00:10:23.870
and who is meant to do what, with particular
respect, I think, to the relationship between

00:10:23.870 --> 00:10:29.050
the Department of Health and NHS England.

00:10:29.050 --> 00:10:35.420
There are many details in the paper which
I will not go into now, other than to say

00:10:35.420 --> 00:10:42.390
that Malcolm Grant chaired a Board which looked
specifically at clinical and patient engagement

00:10:42.390 --> 00:10:48.590
infomatics and data and the benefits realisation
that might be achieved from this.

00:10:48.590 --> 00:10:54.300
The Chief Medical Officer chaired another
group with a series of workstreams looking

00:10:54.300 --> 00:11:02.300
at the science, the ethics and data storage
about this; and the paper really describes

00:11:02.300 --> 00:11:08.701
how these groups will work together in the
future.

00:11:08.701 --> 00:11:09.701
&gt;&gt; Thank you very much, Bruce.

00:11:09.701 --> 00:11:13.110
The current outcome of all of that is the
establishment of Genomics England, which is

00:11:13.110 --> 00:11:22.490
a company limited by shares; the company will
be chaired by Sir John Chisholm, former chairman

00:11:22.490 --> 00:11:27.430
of the Medical Research Council, and with
stakeholder representations from the different

00:11:27.430 --> 00:11:28.430
organisations.

00:11:28.430 --> 00:11:33.650
Again, the critical thing is to get absolute
clarity and accountability for this exercise

00:11:33.650 --> 00:11:39.780
and for us to be clear on what are the deliverables
for us as NHS England as a consequence of

00:11:39.780 --> 00:11:40.910
this investment.

00:11:40.910 --> 00:11:45.860
&gt;&gt; It sounds like science fiction.

00:11:45.860 --> 00:11:48.840
It is amazing.

00:11:48.840 --> 00:11:55.200
Just to be clear: are we talking about the
development of bespoke medicines and interventions

00:11:55.200 --> 00:11:56.740
for individuals?

00:11:56.740 --> 00:12:07.150
I just wonder about some of the ethical issues
around race, which I have read about in other

00:12:07.150 --> 00:12:12.100
areas, and whether that has been considered
because that has implications for resource

00:12:12.100 --> 00:12:13.100
allocation.

00:12:13.100 --> 00:12:17.930
I just wonder about whether that is something
that has been thought about or whether it

00:12:17.930 --> 00:12:24.560
will be thought about, because it has huge
implications if that is one of the products.

00:12:24.560 --> 00:12:25.560
&gt;&gt; It is indeed.

00:12:25.560 --> 00:12:30.930
One of Sally Davies's three working groups
has had a very profound consideration of that.

00:12:30.930 --> 00:12:36.550
There are many ethical issues tied into this:
about patient counselling, for example, and

00:12:36.550 --> 00:12:39.830
who owns the data and how it is used.

00:12:39.830 --> 00:12:46.350
So, I think what will be best is if we can
supply Victor with a copy of the other reports.

00:12:46.350 --> 00:12:54.550
One can envisage, I think, a state of science
in ten years' time where patients will go

00:12:54.550 --> 00:12:57.994
out and get their whole genome sequenced;
where it could be in the order of £100 or

00:12:57.994 --> 00:13:04.740
£200 to do it, so they turn up to the GP
with their sequenced genome.

00:13:04.740 --> 00:13:12.210
There are huge implications here for ethics,
for counselling, for advice, for science and

00:13:12.210 --> 00:13:20.760
technology and the training of clinical professionals
for the future.

00:13:20.760 --> 00:13:26.570
&gt;&gt; One of the reasons why the Prime Minister
-- and we should be excited by the opportunities,

00:13:26.570 --> 00:13:33.029
though very mindful of all the ethical issues,
is because the NHS does have the opportunity

00:13:33.029 --> 00:13:38.830
to deliver a world-class data infrastructure.

00:13:38.830 --> 00:13:42.650
Without the clinical reference data you cannot
make sense of the gene sequences and we are

00:13:42.650 --> 00:13:47.570
in a unique position to offer that to science,
but there are many ethical issues in the offer,

00:13:47.570 --> 00:13:51.200
apart from the sort you are mentioning.

00:13:51.200 --> 00:13:53.600
So, I would just say one of the reasons why
we have got involved in this and why we need

00:13:53.600 --> 00:13:58.470
to figure out I think very carefully what
the cost implications of remaining involved

00:13:58.470 --> 00:14:00.370
are relate to the data infrastructure.

00:14:00.370 --> 00:14:07.910
So, there is a whole piece of this that is
all about just maximising the asset value

00:14:07.910 --> 00:14:18.340
of the data that the NHS produces as a by-product
of this work.

00:14:18.340 --> 00:14:24.540
&gt;&gt; I think it is potentially an enormous breakthrough
for the benefit of patients, which is why

00:14:24.540 --> 00:14:29.589
we would like to be world leaders in this:
for the benefit of NHS patients.

00:14:29.589 --> 00:14:33.130
That would be, I think, a major step forward.

00:14:33.130 --> 00:14:35.470
Thank you very much, Bruce, for that brief
report.

00:14:35.470 --> 00:14:45.490
&gt;&gt; Members of the Board will have some hard
copies of slides, and we will have some slides

00:14:45.490 --> 00:14:48.150
that others can see.

00:14:48.150 --> 00:14:58.380
I am going to go through these relatively
quickly, but if we just go to the next slide:

00:14:58.380 --> 00:14:59.380
this is about the 6Cs.

00:14:59.380 --> 00:15:04.570
It is about our nursing vision and strategy;
it is the one that has been agreed by multiple

00:15:04.570 --> 00:15:08.570
stakeholders and by nurses and midwives in
the NHS itself.

00:15:08.570 --> 00:15:13.950
I think we are all very, very clear that we
have had quite a lot of negative publicity

00:15:13.950 --> 00:15:18.680
and bad news stories around the profession
and the way in which they work.

00:15:18.680 --> 00:15:22.730
That has been repeated on several occasions
more recently, and I thought it was really

00:15:22.730 --> 00:15:27.930
important for the Board to see what we are
actually doing, what practical applications

00:15:27.930 --> 00:15:32.830
we have done, to spread good practice and
really engage with the nursing profession.

00:15:32.830 --> 00:15:38.800
So, the next slide just talks a bit about
what 6Cs Live! is.

00:15:38.800 --> 00:15:44.180
We look at it as almost like the engine room
of the 6Cs; it is about how we turn what is

00:15:44.180 --> 00:15:55.840
a vision and strategy into some practical
reality, and to share their best practice,

00:15:55.840 --> 00:16:00.550
to be able to ask questions and to act together
to really drive improvements.

00:16:00.550 --> 00:16:05.220
I will just take you through over the next
through slides very quickly what it looks

00:16:05.220 --> 00:16:06.350
like.

00:16:06.350 --> 00:16:08.720
If you go onto the 6Cs Live!

00:16:08.720 --> 00:16:14.100
Website, which we launched 13 days ago, there
is a welcome page which describes what it

00:16:14.100 --> 00:16:19.510
is about, discusses what the 6Cs are and how
people can access it.

00:16:19.510 --> 00:16:25.040
The next slide just highlights the registration
point; it allows people to go on and register

00:16:25.040 --> 00:16:28.660
to say who they are, where they work and what
they want to do.

00:16:28.660 --> 00:16:34.290
The next slide shows where we have got the
Compassion in Practice implementation plans,

00:16:34.290 --> 00:16:39.060
and the point about this is it means that
anybody, anywhere, that has got access to

00:16:39.060 --> 00:16:41.600
the internet can have a look and see what
we are doing.

00:16:41.600 --> 00:16:46.090
So, members of staff and members of the public
can see what it is we are doing, what we have

00:16:46.090 --> 00:16:50.200
committed to and what are the timescales.

00:16:50.200 --> 00:16:56.330
The next slide highlights weeks of action,
and what we really wanted to do was to be

00:16:56.330 --> 00:17:02.630
able to have some concentrated areas of effort
where we picked a particular subject, something

00:17:02.630 --> 00:17:09.570
that people were keen to look at, hold webinars,
share best practice, have people doing things

00:17:09.570 --> 00:17:16.350
like guest blogs or discussing what it is
they are doing and really concentrating on

00:17:16.350 --> 00:17:22.590
an area that people can then pick up, take
off and look at in their own local circumstances.

00:17:22.590 --> 00:17:27.601
The next slide highlights where we have a
multitude of partner organisations who have

00:17:27.601 --> 00:17:32.610
signed up, and the partners that have signed
up to this so far include people like the

00:17:32.610 --> 00:17:39.210
Royal College of Nursing, the Florence Nightingale
Foundation and individual organisations, trusts

00:17:39.210 --> 00:17:41.360
and community services.

00:17:41.360 --> 00:17:47.470
It also has some of our key stakeholders like
Health Education England, Public Health England

00:17:47.470 --> 00:17:53.520
and people like Patient Opinion, so we have
the ability for Patient Opinion to both take

00:17:53.520 --> 00:17:59.300
information from our website and actually
share it, and to feed into us different opportunities

00:17:59.300 --> 00:18:00.970
that we have.

00:18:00.970 --> 00:18:07.720
The next slide highlights our desire to really
celebrate success.

00:18:07.720 --> 00:18:13.910
It is a careful balance for me between actually
wanting to promote and share some of the really

00:18:13.910 --> 00:18:18.630
good work that so many people are doing in
the NHS, but also recognising that it is not

00:18:18.630 --> 00:18:21.540
good all of the time everywhere and we cannot
be complacent.

00:18:21.540 --> 00:18:27.630
We need to be driving that improvement but
at the same time really supporting staff out

00:18:27.630 --> 00:18:32.650
on the front line, and giving them the ability
to be proud of what they do and to show what

00:18:32.650 --> 00:18:34.510
they do well.

00:18:34.510 --> 00:18:38.250
There is a real risk that morale just gets
more and more battered by lots of bad news

00:18:38.250 --> 00:18:43.420
stories, and we want to try and balance that
so that we can help people to improve, but

00:18:43.420 --> 00:18:46.170
also celebrate where things go well.

00:18:46.170 --> 00:18:53.341
The picture up there is Matt Hodson, who is
a Consultant Respiratory Nurse, who works

00:18:53.341 --> 00:18:57.750
in London who was the Nurse of the Year, and
as part of that celebrating success we are

00:18:57.750 --> 00:19:03.490
asking people to tell us their good news stories
and tell us what they do.

00:19:03.490 --> 00:19:08.480
We have a panel, which Matt is included on,
as is the Nursing Times; we have got Matt

00:19:08.480 --> 00:19:10.390
King, who is a patient champion.

00:19:10.390 --> 00:19:15.420
So, we will be looking at all of those good
news stories and identifying a story of the

00:19:15.420 --> 00:19:19.840
month, which we will publish on our website
and also will be published by the Nursing

00:19:19.840 --> 00:19:21.160
Times.

00:19:21.160 --> 00:19:24.910
We have also got a section in the next slide
that shows our Care Makers; I will not spend

00:19:24.910 --> 00:19:26.600
long on this, I have talked about them before.

00:19:26.600 --> 00:19:31.340
These are student nurses, newly qualified
nurses, who are acting as our ambassadors

00:19:31.340 --> 00:19:32.340
of the 6Cs.

00:19:32.340 --> 00:19:37.600
They are spreading that good practice: they
are talking about it in their universities

00:19:37.600 --> 00:19:39.860
and in their clinical placements.

00:19:39.860 --> 00:19:44.860
Several members of the Board have met the
Care Makers; they are incredibly motivated,

00:19:44.860 --> 00:19:47.040
inspirational individuals.

00:19:47.040 --> 00:19:53.880
Victor actually has still got a Care Makers
sweatshirt that he wore at expo.

00:19:53.880 --> 00:20:00.960
So, we have a really good team there and they
are setting up their own part of the website.

00:20:00.960 --> 00:20:06.200
We aim to have over 1,000 of those by the
end of this year.

00:20:06.200 --> 00:20:09.559
The next slide highlights that we have got
a live Twitter feed.

00:20:09.559 --> 00:20:16.350
Over the last 13 days, we have now got 1,800
followers; not huge numbers, but there has

00:20:16.350 --> 00:20:22.730
been about 224,000 Twitter impressions so
we are getting quite a lot of noise in the

00:20:22.730 --> 00:20:23.730
system.

00:20:23.730 --> 00:20:27.490
Lots of people are tweeting about different
things.

00:20:27.490 --> 00:20:33.080
The sign up shows that people can pledge or
become champions.

00:20:33.080 --> 00:20:40.230
So, in the 13 days since we went live we have
had 1,703 people sign up to pledge to be part

00:20:40.230 --> 00:20:44.770
of the 6Cs campaign and 310 that have said
they want to be champions.

00:20:44.770 --> 00:20:50.090
Those champions take a very active interest:
there are details about them as individuals

00:20:50.090 --> 00:20:54.450
and they really are out there promoting that
really good practice.

00:20:54.450 --> 00:20:59.910
We have communities of practice that are there,
so if you happen to work in a general practice

00:20:59.910 --> 00:21:03.490
and you want to see what the general practice
nurses or doing or you want to see what community

00:21:03.490 --> 00:21:08.270
nurses are doing, you can click onto that
part and find out what those individuals are

00:21:08.270 --> 00:21:10.680
doing elsewhere in the country.

00:21:10.680 --> 00:21:14.660
We actually have a map that talks about where
we are going, so we say what is happening

00:21:14.660 --> 00:21:18.730
in different parts of the country: where I
am going, where are my team going and what

00:21:18.730 --> 00:21:24.170
events are being held all around the place
that people can actually look at and log on

00:21:24.170 --> 00:21:25.170
to.

00:21:25.170 --> 00:21:29.300
Then we have patient and family stories: the
ability for us to link back to what patients

00:21:29.300 --> 00:21:34.090
and families tell us that is good experiences
that they have got around the 6Cs.

00:21:34.090 --> 00:21:40.220
This helps us with our implementation in terms
of being able to use those stories to perhaps

00:21:40.220 --> 00:21:44.309
be part of appraisal systems and the like.

00:21:44.309 --> 00:21:49.350
The last but one slide is really about some
messages that I wanted to give to the Board

00:21:49.350 --> 00:21:50.720
and those watching.

00:21:50.720 --> 00:21:55.910
This is about disseminating the 6Cs to teams,
it is about spreading it not only amongst

00:21:55.910 --> 00:21:59.580
ourselves in NHS England but more widely out
in the system.

00:21:59.580 --> 00:22:03.250
I am encouraging people to sign up and pledge;
you do not have to be a nurse to do it.

00:22:03.250 --> 00:22:05.390
Anybody can sign up and pledge to the 6Cs.

00:22:05.390 --> 00:22:08.970
The 6Cs are applicable to absolutely everybody.

00:22:08.970 --> 00:22:12.780
Asking people to blog if they want to; the
website is there.

00:22:12.780 --> 00:22:28.990
I am going to end with a photograph of David,
who launched 6Cs in Wolverhampton on 5th July

00:22:28.990 --> 00:22:35.490
with a cake to a group of staff.

00:22:35.490 --> 00:22:39.750
I think we have moved quite a long way since
then, even though it is not a very healthy

00:22:39.750 --> 00:22:40.750
looking cake.

00:22:40.750 --> 00:22:41.750
Thank you very much, and I am very happy to
take any questions.

00:22:41.750 --> 00:22:43.070
&gt;&gt; Jane, thank you very much; that is terrific.

00:22:43.070 --> 00:22:51.150
I think it just brings home to us again and
again and again the sheer importance of nursing

00:22:51.150 --> 00:22:52.150
as a profession.

00:22:52.150 --> 00:22:56.580
It was brought home to us obviously with Mid
Staffs, with the Keogh 14 if I could call

00:22:56.580 --> 00:23:01.620
them that and, of course, Julia Neuberger's
review of the Liverpool care pathway.

00:23:01.620 --> 00:23:07.190
I do not think the profession has ever had
a higher profile and in need of the attention

00:23:07.190 --> 00:23:11.900
that you are giving in your professional leadership
role, so I think we all appreciate enormously

00:23:11.900 --> 00:23:16.169
the 6Cs initiative and we will do everything
that we can to support it.

00:23:16.169 --> 00:23:22.240
&gt;&gt; Just remembering to celebrate all the fantastic
stuff that happens out there every day as

00:23:22.240 --> 00:23:24.500
well; that is the trick.

00:23:24.500 --> 00:23:30.940
It is how we give fantastic people who do
fantastic work the time and the space to do

00:23:30.940 --> 00:23:34.850
their jobs in the way they want to do them,
and so to celebrate their success but also

00:23:34.850 --> 00:23:35.850
create the environment.

00:23:35.850 --> 00:23:38.730
&gt;&gt; That is a really key part of the Compassion
in Practice strategy.

00:23:38.730 --> 00:23:43.190
There was a significant amount of work around
creating the environment, the culture and

00:23:43.190 --> 00:23:49.640
the support to enable staff to be able to
give that care.

00:23:49.640 --> 00:23:53.941
&gt;&gt; At the risk of prolonging what is already
a long meeting, I just wanted to say that

00:23:53.941 --> 00:24:00.820
there is a connection between the work that
Bruce has done and the 6Cs in that the 6Cs

00:24:00.820 --> 00:24:05.800
in itself could be an intervention; it could
also help accelerate.

00:24:05.800 --> 00:24:14.590
The 6Cs should be a normative experience rather
than a structured programme, so it is how

00:24:14.590 --> 00:24:17.529
do we get there that is the question.

00:24:17.529 --> 00:24:22.880
&gt;&gt; If the Liverpool care pathway story was
that in some places there was not care and

00:24:22.880 --> 00:24:26.809
compassion, you can change the pathway but
if you do not do this stuff you do not get

00:24:26.809 --> 00:24:29.429
anywhere; it all kind of feeds into itself.

00:24:29.429 --> 00:24:34.690
&gt;&gt; Well, those, I think, are tremendously
important messages on which to conclude the

00:24:34.690 --> 00:24:35.690
meeting.

00:24:35.690 --> 00:24:40.470
The only other item of business that I have
got is to report that our next meeting will

00:24:40.470 --> 00:24:44.250
be here on 13th September.

00:24:44.250 --> 00:24:49.350
The Board will now go into a private meeting;
I would ask you to support the appropriate

00:24:49.350 --> 00:24:55.210
resolution to do that and to bring to a close
this public meeting.

00:24:55.210 --> 00:24:59.190
May I thank, once again, all the members of
the public who have joined us today and thank

00:24:59.190 --> 00:25:02.950
you for your patience in what has been a long
but not dull meeting.

00:25:02.950 --> 00:25:05.150
Thank you; the meeting is closed.

